      "3_4": "DNA holds the instructions for an organism's traits, and a gene is a section of DNA. A pea plant has two alleles, or versions of a gene, for flower color. The alleles it carries are its genotype. A purple allele is dominant and a white allele is recessive, so a plant with one of each has purple flowers. The purple flower is its phenotype, the observable physical trait. Sometimes a mutation, which is a change in the DNA sequence, creates a new allele. A new allele can change a trait in the next generation. Over many generations, mutations and inheritance explain why traits vary. Scientists model these patterns with diagrams.",
