      "3_4": "Earth's outer crust is broken into massive slabs called tectonic plates. The plates move very slowly, only a few centimeters each year. Beneath the crust is the mantle, a hot layer of rock that flows slowly, and this flow helps move the plates. Sometimes one plate sinks beneath another plate, and this process is called subduction. Subduction can cause powerful earthquakes and volcanoes. Earthquakes and volcanoes are not spread evenly around the world. They happen mostly near the edges of plates, where plates meet. Scientists use maps of earthquakes and volcanoes to find plate boundaries. These maps show a dynamic crust that is always changing slowly.",
      "5_6": "Earth's outer crust is broken into massive slabs called tectonic plates, which move very slowly, only a few centimeters each year. Beneath the crust lies the mantle, a hot layer of rock that flows slowly, and this flow helps move the plates. Where two plates meet, one plate sometimes sinks beneath another, a process called subduction. Subduction can produce powerful earthquakes and volcanoes. Earthquakes and volcanoes are not spread evenly around the world, because most of them occur near plate boundaries. Scientists map where earthquakes and volcanoes happen, and the pattern outlines the edges of plates. They also study seafloor spreading, which adds new crust where plates move apart. Together, this evidence explains why Earth's crust is dynamic rather than fixed. The slow movement of the mantle and plates reshapes oceans and mountains over millions of years. Understanding these processes helps communities prepare for earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. Each boundary type produces its own pattern of hazards.",
