      "1_2": "Plants make food in a chloroplast. A chloroplast is the plant cell part where photosynthesis happens. Plants use light, water, and carbon dioxide. They make glucose and oxygen. Glucose is a simple sugar that stores chemical energy. Your cells use glucose and oxygen to release energy. They also make carbon dioxide and water. Atoms are rearranged, not created or destroyed. This idea is called conservation.",
      "3_4": "Plants make food by photosynthesis, which happens in the chloroplast, a plant cell organelle. Plants use light energy, water, and carbon dioxide to make glucose and oxygen. Glucose is a simple sugar molecule that stores chemical energy. When you eat food, your cells use glucose and oxygen to release energy. This process is cellular respiration, and it produces carbon dioxide and water. In both processes, atoms are only rearranged and never created or destroyed. This idea is called conservation. Because of conservation, the carbon in glucose once came from carbon dioxide in the air. Plants and animals both depend on these two processes.",
      "5_6": "Plants make food by photosynthesis, which happens in the chloroplast, a plant cell organelle. During photosynthesis, plants use light energy, water, and carbon dioxide to make glucose and oxygen. Glucose is a simple sugar molecule that stores chemical energy. When you eat food, your cells use glucose and oxygen in cellular respiration to release energy, and this process produces carbon dioxide and water. In both processes, atoms are only rearranged and never created or destroyed, an idea called conservation. Because of conservation, the carbon in glucose once came from carbon dioxide in the air, and the carbon you exhale returns to the air to be used again. Students investigate these reactions at the molecular level to trace carbon and energy cycling through ecosystems. Energy, unlike matter, flows through an ecosystem and is eventually released as heat. Sunlight is the original source of most of that energy. Tracing atoms and energy shows how living things depend on each other.",
