      "3_4": "In 1787, leaders wrote the Constitution, but they did not agree on everything. They reached a compromise, which is an agreement where each side gives up something. One compromise gave each state two senators, while the number of House members depends on population. The Constitution also set up federalism, a division of power between national and state governments. For the Constitution to become law, nine of the 13 states had to ratify it, which means formally approve it. Federalists supported it, but Anti-Federalists worried that the national government would be too strong. Their debate shaped the Early Republic. Compromise made agreement possible.",
      "5_6": "In 1787, leaders at the Constitutional Convention wrote a new plan of government, but they did not agree on everything. They reached a compromise, which is an agreement where each side gives up something. One compromise gave each state two senators, while the number of House members depends on population. The Constitution also established federalism, a division of power between national and state governments. For the Constitution to become law, nine of the 13 states had to ratify it, which means formally approve it. Federalists supported ratification, but Anti-Federalists worried that the national government would become too strong and wanted protections for rights. These debates, along with later ideas such as judicial review, shaped the Early Republic. Compromise made agreement possible, but it also left some hard questions unanswered. Students analyze these arguments to understand why the Constitution looks the way it does. Each side had to weigh what it wanted against what it could accept.",
